    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Corbin Carroll** standing as the clear focal point of our offensive strategy. Boasting an elite efficiency score of 87.13, **Corbin Carroll** draws a favorable matchup against Paredes, whose 4.94 xERA invites aggression. The combination of a strong park factor and a premium #3 lineup slot makes him our most dangerous weapon for both counting stats and stolen bases. Close behind him, **James Wood** (67.45) offers immense upside at the top of the order; his elite 0.404 xwOBA against a vulnerable Martinez indicates a high probability of creating runs, despite a slight park penalty. We are also leaning on **Gabriel Moreno** (69.89) and **Brice Turang** (66.82) as core plays; **Brice Turang** remains a primary target for stolen base production today, as Elder\u2019s slow delivery provides a distinct green light on the basepaths that elevates his score well above his base projection.\n\nOur rotation decisions were guided by the necessity to maintain our stolen base profile while navigating a difficult matchup against Detmers. This directly impacted our decision to start **Henry Bolte** (43.28) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (49.01) despite the risk posed by Detmers\u2019 3.23 xERA; their projected output outweighed the alternatives on our bench. The most difficult cut of the day was **Max Muncy** (44.18), who narrowly missed the threshold. While **Max Muncy** carries inherent power, the combination of his low #9 lineup position and the elite arm of Detmers tipped the optimizer toward players with better platoon advantages and higher situational scoring. We are also keeping a close watch on the status of **Vinnie Pasquantino**, who remains on the IL, while ensuring that lower-floor options like **Cole Young** (39.20) stay benched to protect our game cap usage. With **Austin Riley** (65.64) offering a strong floor against Gasser, we are positioned to capitalize on favorable platoon matchups despite the looming threat of high-skill pitching from Detmers.",
